          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How can I tell from the tabular list that a code is a combination code rather than a plain one?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched only partly answer this. They explain the general idea of a combination code and give examples, but they do not list the exact wording of the tabular notes, so I can't give you a full rule set.\n\n**What the sources do say:**\n\n- **The tabular list governs.** The alphabetic index points you to a code, but the tabular list carries the instructional notes, so you check the code there before using it. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n- **A combination code joins two conditions into one code.** Type 2 diabetes with kidney disease is an example. The combination code names the association, and an additional code gives the detail, such as the stage of kidney disease or the site and depth of a foot ulcer. (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*)\n- **Some combination codes need a second code.** Hypertension with chronic kidney disease uses a combination code, and a second code gives the stage of kidney disease. Hypertensive heart disease with heart failure uses a combination code, with a heart failure code added. (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*; *Coding heart failure: I50*)\n- **The word \"with\" is a convention.** When the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a link unless the record says the two are unrelated. (*Coding type 2 diabetes*)\n- **Excludes1 means the codes are never reported together.** (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions*)\n\n**What the sources do not cover:** the specific \"use additional code\" and \"code also\" wording in the tabular list, and how to tell those notes apart. The passage on code structure stops before it reaches that material.\n\nFor those rules, the official ICD-10-CM Tabular List and its guidelines are the right place to check. A certified medical coder can also walk you through a specific case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
